1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,819
This is a download from BBC Learning English.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,419
To find out more, visit our website.

3
00:00:12,099 --> 00:00:15,539
Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English.

4
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:16,440
I'm Sam.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:17,379
And I'm Neil.

6
00:00:17,679 --> 00:00:23,379
In 1436 in Germany, Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press,

7
00:00:23,579 --> 00:00:28,399
a machine capable of making many copies of the same page of text.

8
00:00:28,399 --> 00:00:32,920
Ever since, printing has been used around the world to produce books,

9
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:34,960
newspapers and magazines.

10
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,960
Printing technology has come a long way since Gutenberg's time,

11
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,259
but even today's most advanced laser printers have only printed flat,

12
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,320
two-dimensional objects until now.

13
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:52,240
In this programme, we're discussing 3D printers, printers which can build solid,

14
00:00:52,259 --> 00:00:57,000
three-dimensional objects out of a variety of materials, including plastic,

15
00:00:57,299 --> 00:00:58,420
concrete and metal.

16
00:00:58,619 --> 00:01:03,060
Now, Neil, when you say a printer that can make solid objects,

17
00:01:03,259 --> 00:01:06,040
I guess you're not talking about a normal printer.

18
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:07,200
That's right, Sam.

19
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:12,359
These large and complex 3D printers work in a completely different way.

20
00:01:12,579 --> 00:01:17,939
Unlike a sculptor who chips away at a block of stone to reveal a shape underneath,

21
00:01:17,939 --> 00:01:24,239
3D printers work in the opposite way, building up physical objects by adding material

22
00:01:24,239 --> 00:01:25,640
layer on layer.

23
00:01:25,879 --> 00:01:31,519
And the ability to print objects in this way is providing solutions to many problems,

24
00:01:31,519 --> 00:01:33,280
as we'll be finding out.

25
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,299
But first I have a question for you, Neil.

26
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:41,260
Before Johannes Gutenberg invented his printing press, copies of texts were made by block

27
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:45,060
printing, using hand-carved wooden blocks pressed into ink.

28
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:49,500
So, what was the oldest known text to be printed this way?

29
00:01:49,659 --> 00:01:55,239
Was it A, a religious teaching, B, a cooking recipe, or C, a love letter?

30
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,519
I think it might have been a recipe.

31
00:01:57,859 --> 00:02:00,780
OK, Neil, I'll reveal the answer later in the programme.

32
00:02:01,019 --> 00:02:06,359
The idea of printing solid objects is not new, but it was only after the millennium that

33
00:02:06,359 --> 00:02:09,300
tech companies began to realise how it could be done.

34
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:14,580
Here's Professor Mark Miodovnik, a material scientist at University College London,

35
00:02:15,159 --> 00:02:19,259
explaining more to BBC World Service programme, People Fixing the World.

36
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:24,500
As the millennium turned, patents expired, and that meant people started making really

37
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,740
cheap 3D printers, and people started mucking about with them

38
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:30,419
and going, hold on a minute, it's not just an industrial tool,

39
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:33,560
you can put them in schools, you can put them in universities.

40
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:36,219
Oh, it's actually really great for prototyping.

41
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:39,599
And then people got excited about it, and it became the answer to everything.

42
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:41,680
Was going to be 3D printed.

43
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:46,120
After the year 2000, 3D printers suddenly got much cheaper,

44
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:51,539
and tech companies started mucking about with them, spending time playing with them in

45
00:02:51,539 --> 00:02:52,340
a fun way.

46
00:02:52,379 --> 00:02:55,379
They realised that 3D printers had many uses.

47
00:02:55,740 --> 00:03:00,259
For example, they discovered that 3D printers were great at making prototypes,

48
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:05,620
models of a product that can be tested, improved and used to develop better products.

49
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:09,659
Professor Miodovnik thinks these tech companies were surprised at how

50
00:03:09,659 --> 00:03:11,419
useful 3D printing was.

51
00:03:11,699 --> 00:03:17,360
He uses the phrase, hold on a minute, to express this surprise or disbelief.

52
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:22,680
In fact, it turned out that 3D printers were excellent at making bespoke things,

53
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:26,259
objects which are made specially for a particular person.

54
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:31,819
One area which 3D printing dramatically improved was medical prosthetics,

55
00:03:31,819 --> 00:03:36,680
artificial body parts made specially for someone who has lost an arm,

56
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:38,680
a leg or a foot, for example.

57
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:43,560
In 2021, Stephen Verz, who lost an eye in a childhood accident,

58
00:03:43,879 --> 00:03:48,780
became the first person to be fitted with a 3D printed prosthetic eye.

59
00:03:49,039 --> 00:03:53,099
It's prosthetic, so the new eye doesn't restore Stephen's sight,

60
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:55,719
but it has boosted his confidence.

61
00:03:56,379 --> 00:04:02,219
Surgeon Mandeep Sagu led the team at Morsefield Hospital that operated on Stephen's eye.

62
00:04:02,479 --> 00:04:07,400
Here he is explaining more to BBC World Service's People Fixing the World.

63
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:11,680
In many countries, particularly in the developed world, there are facilities for custom

64
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:14,460
making a prosthetic eye to match the other eye.

65
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:19,180
And that's an artisan process which is very time consuming

66
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:22,699
and requires real artistry on the part of the ocularist.

67
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:25,959
Is the person who fits the prosthetic eye.

68
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:32,180
And so what we have been developing is a technique to automate the whole process.

69
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:36,379
Even before 3D printers, prosthetic eyes were custom made,

70
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:42,339
a word similar to bespoke, which means specially made according to a particular person's

71
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:43,139
requirements.

72
00:04:43,379 --> 00:04:48,680
But the traditional way of making artificial eyes by hand is very time consuming.

73
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:50,939
It takes a lot of time to do.

74
00:04:50,939 --> 00:04:55,980
Nowadays, 3D printing can complete the whole process in just 30 minutes.

75
00:04:56,180 --> 00:04:58,540
It's great to see technology helping people.

76
00:04:58,779 --> 00:05:03,279
And amazing how far new inventions like 3D printers have come since

77
00:05:03,279 --> 00:05:05,120
the days of Johannes Gutenberg.

78
00:05:05,279 --> 00:05:09,439
Speaking of which, Neil, it's time to reveal the answer to my question.

79
00:05:09,699 --> 00:05:10,500
Right.

80
00:05:10,199 --> 00:05:14,759
You asked me about the earliest known text to have been printed using wooden blocks.

81
00:05:14,899 --> 00:05:17,779
And I guessed it was a cooking recipe.

82
00:05:17,779 --> 00:05:18,939
So was I right?

83
00:05:18,939 --> 00:05:21,519
You were wrong, I'm afraid, Neil.

84
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:25,959
The oldest known wooden block print was actually a religious text,

85
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:27,860
the Buddha's Diamond Sutra.

86
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:33,339
OK, let's recap the vocabulary from this programme, starting with mucking about,

87
00:05:33,399 --> 00:05:38,420
an informal way to say playing with something carelessly, not for a serious reason.

88
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:42,740
A prototype is a model of a product that can be tested, improved

89
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:44,800
and used to develop a better product.

90
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:49,379
The phrase hold on a minute can be used to express surprise or disbelief.

91
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:53,740
Prosthetics refer to artificial body parts such as arms, legs,

92
00:05:53,899 --> 00:05:57,860
feet or eyes, which are used to replace a missing natural part.

93
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,540
The words bespoke and custom made describe something specially made for a particular

94
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:04,340
person.

95
00:06:04,379 --> 00:06:08,899
And finally, if something is time consuming, it takes a lot of time to do.

96
00:06:09,199 --> 00:06:10,220
Goodbye for now.

97
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:11,020
Goodbye.

98
00:06:11,459 --> 00:06:14,339
Six Minute English from the BBC.

